\mt1 Psalms

\c 13

\s1 How Long

\d For the director of music. A psalm of David.\f + \fr 13:0 \fk KONTEXT \fq Psalm 13 in context \ft Psalm 13 is the shortest complete lament in the Psalter — and the paradigm specimen of the genre (Gunkel's "Musterbeispiel"). Six verses. Three movements: accusation (vv. 1–2), command (vv. 3–4), trust (vv. 5–6). Its defining structural feature is the fourfold "how long?" — a staircase that escalates from divine forgetting, to divine hiding, to inner agony, to the enemy's domination. What makes this psalm remarkable is what happens between verses 4 and 5: the psalmist shifts from terror to trust without anything changing. No oracle, no answered prayer, no external resolution. The act of praying is itself the transformation. — Psalm 12 ends with God's promise: "Now I will arise." Psalm 13 opens: "How long?" The Psalter places lament immediately after divine promise — teaching that honest complaint does not contradict faith. It accompanies faith in the gap between promise and fulfillment.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 1 How long, Eternal — will you forget me forever.\f + \fr 13:1a \fk TEXT \fq How long \ft The Hebrew *ʿad-ʾānâ* appears four times in two verses — the psalm's defining structural feature. This phrase is rarer and more urgent than the usual "how long?" (*ʿad-matay*); in the Psalter it occurs only here (four times) and in Psalm 62:3 (once, addressed to humans). The fourfold repetition is a staircase of escalating accusation: (1) divine forgetting (passive neglect), (2) divine face-hiding (active withdrawal), (3) inner cognitive-emotional agony, (4) the enemy's triumph. The monotony is intentional — suffering that repeats itself without resolution.\f*\f + \fr 13:1b \fk TEXT \fq forget me \ft The Hebrew *shakah* (to forget) is not absent-mindedness. In covenant language, "forgetting" is the violation of a binding relationship. YHWH entered a covenant with Israel; to forget is to fail that obligation. The psalmist is not whimpering — they are filing a charge: *you made a commitment and you are not keeping it*.\f*
\q1 How long will you hide your face from me.\f + \fr 13:1c \fk KULTUR \fq hide your face \ft In the ancient world, God's "face" (*panim*) was the seat of blessing and protection. The priestly blessing (Numbers 6:24–26) asks God to "make his face shine upon you and be gracious to you." When God hides the face, the protection disappears — not as passive absence but as active withdrawal. The escalation from "forget" to "hide your face" is from neglect to deliberate turning away.\f*
\b
\q1
\v 2 How long must I churn these thoughts in me,\f + \fr 13:2a \fk TEXT \fq churning thoughts \ft The Hebrew *ʿēṣôt* means deliberations, agonized plans, obsessive rumination — NOT "sorrow" (which is the separate word *yāgôn* in the next clause). The psalmist names two distinct torments: (1) thoughts that circle without resolution (cognitive exhaustion) and (2) grief that weighs on the heart (emotional pain). Many translations collapse these into one; the Hebrew keeps them deliberately separate.\f*
\q1 grief in my heart, day after day.
\q1 How long will my enemy loom over me.\f + \fr 13:2b \fk KULTUR \fq enemy looms over me \ft The Hebrew *yārûm ʿōyĕvî ʿālay* — literally "my enemy rises high over me." The "enemy" is singular but collective: under Hossfeld and Zenger's reading of Psalms 11–14 as the prayers of the oppressed poor, this is not an abstract adversary but a socially concrete figure — the powerful person enabled by divine inaction to exploit the vulnerable. The word *ʿālay* ("over me") will return in v. 6 with reversed meaning — see note there.\f*
\b
\q1
\v 3 Look at me. Answer me, Eternal, my God.
\q1 Light up my eyes\f + \fr 13:3a \fk TEXT \fq light up my eyes \ft "Enlighten my eyes" is bodily language for rescue from death. In the ancient Near East, bright/shining eyes signaled vitality; dimming eyes signaled the approach of death. The psalmist is not asking for spiritual insight — they are asking to be pulled back from physical dying. The Assyrian elegy K 890 uses identical body-language for the same experience of divine absence.\f*
\q1 before I sleep — death.\f + \fr 13:3b \fk TEXT \fq sleep — death \ft The Hebrew places these two words in naked juxtaposition: *pen-ʾîshan hammāwet* — "lest I sleep, the-death." No connecting syntax. Robert Alter: "The poet uses a jolting elliptical form... that is worth preserving in translation." This is not "the sleep of death" (a smooth genitive) but an interruption — "sleep" and then "death" crashes into the line, as if the thought itself is dying mid-sentence.\f*
\b
\q1
\v 4 Don't let my enemy say, "I won."
\q1 Don't let them celebrate my fall.\f + \fr 13:4 \fk KULTUR \fq enemy's celebration \ft The psalmist's fear is not primarily about social humiliation. If the enemy triumphs and gloats, it would constitute a disproof of YHWH's covenant fidelity — evidence that God abandons even those who belong to him. The enemy's gloating is a theological crisis, not a status problem. James Mays: "The enemy wears the mask of death."\f*
\b
\q1
\v 5 But I —\f + \fr 13:5a \fk TEXT \fq But I \ft The Hebrew *waʾănî* — adversative "but" plus emphatic "I" — is the psalm's single most important moment. Nothing has changed externally. No oracle has been spoken, no circumstance has shifted. The psalmist makes a decision. The trust that follows is not emotional optimism; it is a volitional act (*bāṭaḥ* = to lean one's weight on, to commit oneself deliberately). The perfect tense "I have trusted" signals a completed action — the decision is already made in the act of speaking it.\f*
\q1 I have trusted in your faithful love.\f + \fr 13:5b \fk TEXT \fq faithful love \ft Hebrew *ḥesed* — the most important theological term in this psalm and one of the most significant in the Hebrew Bible. *Ḥesed* is not generic love, mercy, or kindness. It is the specific quality of loyalty that belongs to a covenant relationship: the love that a partner owes because of a binding commitment. The psalmist's trust is grounded not in feeling better but in a claim on YHWH's own character: *you are a God who keeps faith; I am holding you to that*.\f*
\q1 My heart will sing because you rescue me.
\b
\q1
\v 6 I will sing to the Eternal,
\q1 because he has been good to me.\f + \fr 13:6 \fk TEXT \fq has been good to me \ft Two remarkable features in this closing line: (1) The Hebrew verb *gāmal* is past tense — "he has dealt fully with me" — spoken before the rescue arrives. This is proleptic praise: faith speaks of the future as already accomplished. The grammar itself performs the certainty. (2) The word *ʿālay* ("to/for me") is the same word that appeared in v. 2 as "over me" (the enemy's domination). Same preposition, same suffix, reversed direction. The psalm's entire emotional arc — from being dominated to being cared for — is encoded in one repeated syllable.\f*
